Hakodate Branch","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":41.771704,"lo":140.730574,"d":2488,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-16:00","w":"http://www.oshima-shinkin.jp/","ph":"+81 138 23 7191"},{"n":"市立函館病院","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":41.805434,"lo":140.729995,"d":2550},{"n":"Port of Hakodate","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.8072,"lo":140.7106,"d":2135,"ds":"The Port of Hakodate (函館港, Hakodate-kō) is one of the main ports in northern Japan, located in Hakodate, on the northern island of Hokkaido. The port was damaged in the March 2011 earthquake.","wpu":"Port of Hakodate","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Hakodate_Port_Hokkaido_Japan01s5.jpg/960px-Hakodate_Port_Hokkaido_Japan01s5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mount Hakodate","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.758889,"lo":140.704444,"d":3269,"ds":"Mount Hakodate (函館山, Hakodate-yama) is an inactive volcanic mountain in Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan. The mountain is renowned for its view of the surrounding bay and city. The Michelin Green Guide: Japan gave the experience 3/3 stars in a review, placing it on par with mountain views of Naples and Hong Kong.","wpu":"Mount Hakodate","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Kanemori_Red_Brick_Warehouse_Hakodate_Hokkaido_pref_Japan01n.jpg/960px-Kanemori_Red_Brick_Warehouse_Hakodate_Hokkaido_pref_Japan01n.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hakodate City Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.755972,"lo":140.714667,"d":3582,"ds":"Hakodate City Museum (市立函館博物館, Shiritsu Hakodate Hakubutsukan) is a museum of history and natural history in Hakodate Park, Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan. The forerunner of the current museum, the Hakodate Provisional Museum, building one, opened in May 1879, the second building in 1884, and the third building (later demolished) in 1891.","wpu":"Hakodate City Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Hakodate_City_Museum.jpg/960px-Hakodate_City_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hakodate Museum of Art, Hokkaido","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.793417,"lo":140.754639,"d":3749,"ds":"Hakodate Museum of Art, Hokkaido (北海道立函館美術館, Hokkaidō-ritsu Hakodate Bikutsukan) opened in Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan in 1986. The collection focuses on works from southern Hokkaidō, including paintings by Kakizaki Hakyō and calligraphy by Kaneko Ōtei (金子鴎亭), and special exhibitions are also mounted.","wpu":"Hakodate Museum of Art, Hokkaido","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/%28JPN-Hokkaido%29_Hakodate_Museum_of_Art_2026-06-08.jpg/960px-%28JPN-Hokkaido%29_Hakodate_Museum_of_Art_2026-06-08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hakodate City Central Library","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.798318,"lo":140.753518,"d":3785,"ds":"Hakodate City Central Library (函館市中央図書館, Hakodate-shi Chūō Toshokan) is a public library in Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan. The library is known in particular for its important collection of northern materials and for its Takuboku Library (啄木文庫).","wpu":"Hakodate City Central Library","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/%28JPN-Hokkaido%29_Hakodate_City_Central_Library_2026-06-08.jpg/960px-%28JPN-Hokkaido%29_Hakodate_City_Central_Library_2026-06-08.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hakodate Hachimangū","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.7539,"lo":140.7101,"d":3791,"ds":"Hakodate Hachiman Shrine (函館八幡宮, Hakodate Hachimangū) is a Shinto shrine located in Hakodate, Hokkaido. It is a Hachiman shrine, dedicated to the kami Hachiman. It is also a Sōja shrine that enshrines all the deities of its region, although it technically does not have a province.","wpu":"Hakodate Hachimangū","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hakodate_Hachiman_Shrine_in_Nov_2024a.jpg/960px-Hakodate_Hachiman_Shrine_in_Nov_2024a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Republic of Ezo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.796944,"lo":140.756944,"d":4016,"ds":"The Ezo Island Government (蝦夷島政府, Ezogashima Seifu), also known as the Republic of Ezo (蝦夷共和國, Ezo Kyōwakoku), was a short-lived separatist state established in 1869 on the island of Ezo, now Hokkaido, by a part of the former military of the Tokugawa shogunate at the end of the Bakumatsu period in Japan.","wpu":"Republic of Ezo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Republic_of_Ezo_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg/960px-Republic_of_Ezo_%28orthographic_projection%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yunokawa Onsen (Hokkaido)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.77875,"lo":140.78258,"d":6105,"ds":"The Yunokawa Onsen (湯の川温泉) is a well known onsen in Japan on the northern island of Hokkaidō. It is located on the outskirts of Hakodate city close to Hakodate Airport.","wpu":"Yunokawa Onsen (Hokkaido)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/27/Yunokawa_Onsen_Hakodate_Hokkaido_Japan01s3.jpg/960px-Yunokawa_Onsen_Hakodate_Hokkaido_Japan01s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hokuto, Hokkaido","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.824167,"lo":140.652778,"d":6218,"ds":"Hokuto (北斗市, Hokuto-shi) is a city in the center of the Oshima Subprefecture in Japan's northern island of Hokkaido. It serves as a bedroom community for Hakodate, to the east of the city. Hokuto was formed on February 1, 2006, from merging the town of Kamiiso, from Kamiiso District, and the town of Ōno, from Kameda District.","wpu":"Hokuto, Hokkaido","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Hokuto_City_Hall.jpg/960px-Hokuto_City_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shiryōkaku","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.825694,"lo":140.770694,"d":6547,"ds":"Shiryōkaku (四稜郭) (literally, \"four-point fort\") is a fort in the city of Hakodate in southern Hokkaidō, Japan. It was constructed in April 1869, during the Battle of Hakodate, three kilometres to the northeast of Goryōkaku by 200 soldiers of the former Tokugawa shogunate and 100 local villagers, likely under the direction of Ōtori Keisuke.","wpu":"Shiryōkaku","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Shiry%C5%8Dkaku.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kōsetsu-en","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.793056,"lo":140.803333,"d":7758,"ds":"Kōsetsu-en (香雪園) is a garden that fuses Japanese and Western styles in Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan. It was constructed in 1898 (or alternatively 1895) by a prominent merchant by the name of Iwafune. The gardens are a nationally designated Place of Scenic Beauty.","wpu":"Kōsetsu-en","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Miharashi_Park_%28Kosetsuen%29_Entei_20181019-001.jpg/960px-Miharashi_Park_%28Kosetsuen%29_Entei_20181019-001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hokkaidō Tōshō-gū","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.83865,"lo":140.78515,"d":8396,"ds":"Hokkaido Tōshō-gū (北海道東照宮) is a Shinto shrine in Hakodate, Hokkaido Prefecture, Japan. It enshrines the first Shōgun of the Tokugawa Shogunate, Tokugawa Ieyasu. It was previously known as Hakodate Tōshō-gū (函館東照宮).","wpu":"Hokkaidō Tōshō-gū","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/%E5%8C%97%E6%B5%B7%E9%81%93%E6%9D%B1%E7%85%A7%E5%AE%AE.jpg/960px-%E5%8C%97%E6%B5%B7%E9%81%93%E6%9D%B1%E7%85%A7%E5%AE%AE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Our Lady of the Angels Trappistine Abbey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.78785,"lo":140.822514,"d":9328,"ds":"Our Lady of the Angels Trappistine Abbey (天使の聖母トラピスチヌ修道院, Tenshi No Seibo Torasupichinu Shūdōin) is a women's abbey of Trappists located in the outskirts of Hakodate, Hokkaido, Japan. This is the first women contemplative order of Japan, colloquially known as \"The Anjel Garden\"(Tenshien).","wpu":"Our Lady of the Angels Trappistine Abbey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Trappistine_Convent_202405.jpg/960px-Trappistine_Convent_202405.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shinoridate","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":41.765825,"lo":140.822251,"d":9629,"ds":"The site of Shinoridate (志苔館跡, Shinoridate ato) in Hakodate, Hokkaidō, Japan, is that once occupied by the Shinori Fort or Fortified Residence (as denoted by the tate or date ending). This was the easternmost of the so-called \"Twelve Garrisons of Southern Hokkaido\", built on the Oshima Peninsula by the Wajin from the fourteenth century.","wpu":"Shinoridate","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Shinoritateato_kuusatsu.jpg/960px-Shinoritateato_kuusatsu.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Hakodate (函館市, Hakodate-shi; Japanese pronunciation: [ha.ko.da.te, ha.ko.da.teꜜ.ɕi]) is a city and port located in Oshima Subprefecture, Hokkaido, Japan. It is the capital city of Oshima Subprefecture. As of January 31, 2024, the city had an estimated population of 239,813 with 138,807 households, and a population density of 354 inhabitants per square kilometre (920/sq mi).","wpu":"Hakodate","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/69/Hakodate_montage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}